<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volodymyr Olgerdovich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1398</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir Olgerdovich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Volodymyr Olgerdovich was a son of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> who ruled the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> as a Lithuanian prince from about 1362 to 1394. He minted his own coins and kept considerable independence until Vytautas removed him in 1395 and gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapyl">Kapyl</a> in compensation. His descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes, later held Kiev again and ruled Slutsk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volodymyr-olgerdovich.jpg" alt="Volodymyr Olgerdovich" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vladimiras_Algirdas._1362-1394.Kiev_mint._Military_standard,_swallowtail_banner_decorated_with_cross_and_pellets_set_on_curved_arm_surmounted_by_cross,_Cyrillic_legend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
